## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design cross-tenant observability with strict data isolation, covering tracing, logging, sampling, and validation.

## Key Concepts

- OpenTelemetry and trace propagation
- Tenant isolation and boundary enforcement
- Per-tenant sampling and data redaction
- Validation in a single-namespace scenario

## Code Example

```javascript
// PII redaction example (logs)
function redactLogEvent(event, tenantId) {
  const safe = {...event};
  if (safe.message) safe.message = safe.message.replace(/\b(email|ssn|password)\b[^:]*: [^,]+/gi, 'PII_REDACTED');
  if (safe.user && safe.user.id) safe.user.id = 'REDACTED';
  return safe;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect and remediate cross-tenant leakage in traces?
- What metrics would you surface to confirm per-tenant isolation is preserved during incidents?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design tenant-aware rate limiting and observability in CNF gateways, a realistic production concern for multi-tenant platforms used by large companies.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant-scoped policy API (REST)
- Sidecar token bucket limiter with TTL cache
- Real-time policy fetch and rotation
- Central audit/log sink and schema

## Code Example

```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(capacity, fillRate) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.fillRate = fillRate;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.lastRefill = Date.now();
  }
  allow(n = 1) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.lastRefill) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.fillRate);
    this.lastRefill = now;
    if (this.tokens >= n) {
      this.tokens -= n;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle policy drift during high load?
- What approaches ensure audit integrity and privacy?
